// see docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute
// for professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
// LABELED WEAKNESS: setup promised and never paid off. Scene 1 plants a
// loaded mystery (a cryptic note: "the key is under the mat, don't wait too
// long") with real fanfare, scene 3 explicitly checks under the mat and
// finds nothing, and then the note is never mentioned again by the plot —
// only referenced once late, by a side character, as something the
// protagonist admits she forgot about. The actual ending resolves an
// unrelated, unforeshadowed question (whether to sell the cabin). Meant to
// read as a plausible early-career draft: the writer clearly knows how to
// plant a hook, just never circles back to spend it.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Dust sheets over the furniture. NORA FIELDING, 30s, stands with estate executor MR. ABERNETHY, 60s, who hands her a small wooden box.

ABERNETHY
Your uncle left specific instructions that this go to you and only you. There's a note inside, too. He was very particular about that note.

NORA
(reading the note aloud)
"The key is under the mat. Don't wait too long." That's it? No explanation at all?

ABERNETHY
That's everything he left me to pass along. He was a man of very few words, even in writing.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - KITCHEN - DAY

Nora's friend PIPPA OYELARAN, 30s, turns the note over in her hands, delighted.

PIPPA
"Don't wait too long." That is the most dramatic thing I've ever read on a sticky note. What do you think it's the key to?

NORA
No idea. But I'm definitely finding out before I do anything else with this cabin.

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - FRONT PORCH - DAY

Nora crouches and lifts the welcome mat by the front door. Nothing underneath but a few dead leaves and a beetle that scurries off.

NORA
Nothing. Maybe he meant a different mat. I'll figure it out later, I guess.

She sets the mat back down and goes back inside without checking anywhere else.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Nora and Pippa pull dust sheets off furniture, sneezing, sorting items into boxes marked KEEP and DONATE.

PIPPA
This dresser's actually beautiful under all this dust. Your uncle had good taste hiding under all the clutter.

NORA
He really did. I forgot how much stuff was even in here.

She tucks the note into a kitchen drawer to keep it safe and gets back to sorting.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - BEDROOM - DAY

Nora finds a shoebox of old photographs under the bed and sits on the floor going through them one by one.

NORA
Look at this one. That's my uncle at the lake, must be forty years ago now.

PIPPA
He looks so happy there. This whole cabin is basically a photo album you can walk around in.

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - DOCK - DAY

Nora and Pippa sit with their feet in the water, a cooler of drinks between them.

PIPPA
Have you thought about what you're actually going to do with the place? Keep it, sell it?

NORA
I don't know yet. Part of me wants to keep coming up here every summer. Part of me knows I barely have time for that.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - LIVING ROOM - DAY

A REAL ESTATE AGENT walks the room with a clipboard while Nora trails behind, half-listening.

AGENT
Lakefront cabins like this move fast in this market. You could list it by the end of the month if you decided to go that route.

NORA
I appreciate the info. I'm still weighing it. Give me a little more time to think.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - ATTIC - DAY

Nora and Pippa sort through more boxes in a cramped, sun-lit attic, sneezing at the dust.

PIPPA
Your uncle really did not believe in throwing anything away, did he.

NORA
Not even a little. Every box up here has at least one thing worth keeping, though.

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - YARD - DAY

Nora sits on a porch step, looking out at the lake, the real estate agent's card still in her hand.

NORA
(to herself)
I think I'm keeping it. It doesn't feel right to let it go to somebody who didn't know him.

She tucks the card into her pocket and looks satisfied with the decision.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - KITCHEN - DAY

Pippa opens the drawer looking for a bottle opener and finds the old note sitting where Nora left it.

PIPPA
Oh, hey — did you ever figure out what this key thing was about?

NORA
Honestly? I completely forgot about that. I should probably ask around about it sometime.

PIPPA
You definitely should. That's a good little mystery to have just sitting in a drawer.

NORA
Yeah. Anyway, should we order pizza before the drive back?

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - PORCH - DAY

Nora locks up the cabin for the season, the note still folded in the kitchen drawer inside, forgotten again already.

NORA
See you next summer, I guess.

She heads down the porch steps toward the car, cabin settled, mystery untouched behind her.
